00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:26.340
(uplifting music)

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.610
- Hello, Crypto Art Land.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.240
It's me, Second Realm AKA Eric Rhodes,

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.940
And I'm here with my guest,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.173
Gary V, AKA, Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:37.173 --> 00:00:39.715
(Gary chuckling)

00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:41.820
- Yours is much more creative,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.110
as a front-facing name.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.710
Mine was very basic.
(Eric chuckling)

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.280
- I've had mine for about 30 years,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.993
but I just started using
it in the art world.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.350
- Love that.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.750
- Yeah, so tell me,
let's get right into it.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.670
I want to hear about your 500 foot view

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.380
of the NFT space,

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.030
and then give me like a prediction

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.210
over the next two years,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.560
where you see things going.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.200
- I overview is that it is inevitable

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.640
and will be some--.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.270
To me this is not a technology

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.750
or a movement or a social norm

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.320
that kind of goes away.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.630
I think there's not a day

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.450
that I live on earth

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.380
for the rest of my life.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.280
That NFT is not a conversation
that is happening.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.080
Similar to internet, you know,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.600
similar to social networks.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.730
Like, I just don't see...

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.500
So like, I view the, you know,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.980
the non-fungible token,
blockchain realities

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.110
of the digitalization of assets far beyond

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.540
the art and collectibles

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.750
that is so easy to understand

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.330
in something that's complicated for most.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.700
I think this obviously goes

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.700
into every transaction
that's not fungible,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.940
you know, so like, selling a home,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.613
you know, a piece of land,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.670
and many, many, many,
many other transactions.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.770
So, I think it's going to affect

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.230
the way people launch music and books.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.200
Like I, myself am rethinking

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.870
a very lucrative book
deal on the table for me.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:20.703
- Yeah.

00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:21.536
- Because now,

00:02:21.536 --> 00:02:22.880
I have a different path in my head

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:23.853
to what I would do.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.350
- Yeah, this digital ownership for you.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.360
Right through the entire process.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.570
- Correct, then really like,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.403
the ability to sell the NFT

00:02:31.403 --> 00:02:32.870
and then the book comes

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.760
but then I have a secondary income stream

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.720
if people are trading the book collectible

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.830
and then the metadata of me saying,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.050
well you're part of my book club

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.950
and, you know, and no audio book

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:42.783
but we can do Q and A,

00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:44.530
like it just changes everything.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.950
- Yeah, one of the things that I think

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.100
that people talk about is,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.130
oh, when it comes to like the collectible,

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.650
the digital collectible,
and then the physical,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.930
is there concern as well.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.680
What happens when I get the digital--,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.120
the physical collectible and, you know,

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.910
the digital one, you know,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:01.960
do they have to be together?

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.500
And I think of them as like,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.900
remember the old Beckett Magazines

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.860
with card inserts.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.940
- Yeah.
- You would keep the cards

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.510
you would hold on to them as collectibles.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.690
But the magazine was in and of itself,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.550
its own physical item.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.910
I see the same thing.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.390
- Yeah, and I see similarities.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:20.850
The way I've been explained
to people is like,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.840
look, that's the most
important part of NFT's

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.840
for me is does the human being,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.700
or the human beings behind the project

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.190
understand how to actually
build a community.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.060
This is why I feel so confident in it,

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.270
Eric, right?

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.640
I have spent 15 years

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.250
building an actual community

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.640
and have been very thoughtful

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.230
to not monetize it in a very inefficient,

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.230
silly, short-term way.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.063
Same thing's going

00:03:46.063 --> 00:03:46.940
to happen with me and with NFTs.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.680
I will launch my own project

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:51.050
and that project will live with me

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:52.850
for the rest of my fucking life.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.460
Not here's a fucking photo of me, buy it,

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.770
and I'm going to sleep.
- [Eric] Right.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.020
- All of the metadata will be the project,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.120
not the actual imagery

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.010
or the short-term transactional nature.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.110
We'll go into that a little bit later,

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:05.990
let me give you the prediction.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:08.970
So, macro view is holy shit,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.280
this feels like '95 and 2005,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.330
'95 for me was (Gary rowdily exclaiming)

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.270
Oh my God, this Internet,

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.450
Worldwide Web, information super highway.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.660
This is forever,

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.240
I'm going to put my liquor store on it.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.240
It's going to change my life.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.163
That happened, 2005 for me was,

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.520
you know, this MySpace Friendster thing,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.160
this--, the Facebook, you know.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.160
Oh my God, February 2006,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.880
this YouTube thing, wait a minute.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.260
This feels a lot cooler

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.590
than message boards and
comments and a blog.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.460
Hey, this is forever.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.700
This is humans communicating

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:43.820
on top of the internet.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.300
Like we do in real life.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.133
This--,

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:47.390
it wasn't even social media at the time

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.690
I called it web 2.0, these web 2.0 sites,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.030
they're going to change the world.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.270
Well, here we are 15 years later,

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.430
and we're still in the
dawn of Social Networks.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.410
Not even--, you know what I mean?

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.840
Like...
- Yes.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.860
- Facebook had a long run, inc.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.930
You know, Tik Tok is a new club

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.000
that like, so, oh right.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.250
We will communicate over
the internet, right?

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.083
- [Eric] Yeah.

00:05:10.083 --> 00:05:11.130
- E-commerce for me in 95 was,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.920
